  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 428 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA pre-frontal trough will cross the area from west to east today, \nbringing chances for showers and a few thunderstorms. SPC has areas \ngenerally along and south of US 24 in a marginal risk for isolated \nstrong-severe storms, but confidence in this occurring is low. If \nstronger storms do develop, they will not be widespread. The main \nthreat would be damaging winds, frequent lightning, and heavy \ndownpours. Area in the marginal risk should have better instability \nas the feature moves through towards the mid-late afternoon hours \n(1500 J/kg), with about 20-30 knots of 0-6 km AGL shear (better \nshear arrives in the late afternoon, but it's disjointed from the \nbetter instability). Plenty of moisture around with dewpoints in the \n60s, 70s. High temperatures will be in the low to mid 80s \nalong/north of US 24, and the upper 80s further south. Heat indices \nin the southern areas could reach into the low-mid 90s during peak \nheating this afternoon.\n\nSoutherly winds will strengthen slightly out of the southwest \nthrough the day, with a period of westerly flow possible briefly\nbehind the passing trough. Waves on Lake MI around St. Joseph, \nMI northward may climb to around 2-3 feet by mid afternoon, and \naround 1-2 feet further south. Have a moderate swim risk for \nLake MI beaches along the Northern Berrien County, MI shoreline \ntoday (including Silver Beach County Park and Tiscornia Park). \nWaves reach around 2-3 feet for Southern Berrien County, MI and \nLa Porte County, IN by Thursday as well with northerly flow \npicking up slightly tonight into Thu. Have a moderate swim risk \nfor both counties accordingly. Today, a moderate to strong south\nto north flowing longshore current is expected, which will lead\nto strong structural currents on the south side of \npiers/breakwalls. Rip currents are possible within the sandbars.\nAll current types are possible on Thursday.\n\nBecoming cooler and less humid behind a passing cold front on \nThursday. Chances (30-50%) for showers and thunderstorms remain \nmainly along/south of US 30 during this period. Severe weather is \nnot expected. It's possible that the front reaches further south at \na quicker pace than currently forecast, which would reduce pops for \nThursday all together. Highs will be around 80 degrees, with lows in \nthe 50s, low 60s.\n\nWhile we will still have weak upper trough overhead on Friday (some \nmodels suggest more riding), surface high pressure remains in \ncontrol with easterly flow in the low levels. Most of the guidance \nremains dry in this period, with the exception being the far south \nwhere some models produce light precip. Left out for now given low \nconfidence. Highs will be around 80 degrees, with lows in the 50s, \nlow 60s.\n\nHighs warm back into low-mid 80s for the weekend into Monday, with \nan upper level ridge Friday night becoming more zonal with time, \nleaving us susceptible to shortwaves kicking off showers and storms. \nHave low chances (20-30%) each day through the period. Better \npotential (30-50% chances) arrive mid-late week as an upper level \nlow drops into the northern plains and a broad surface low \ndeveloping near IA deepens and lifts into Ontario/Quebec. This will \nbring a cold front through sometime between Tue-Wed. Highs remain in \nthe upper 70s, low-mid 80s, coolest Wednesday.",
